H2-1  deterministic contraction on R^2:  F(x) = c R(phi) x + b,  c = 0.8
==============================================================================
  b = 0 (law is rotation-equivariant about 0): fixed point x* = [-0.  0.];  max |x_400 - x*| over starts incl. 1e6: 1.8e-33
  b = (1.3, -0.4) (explicit offset): fixed point x* = [1.400924 0.950654];  max |x_400 - x*| over starts incl. 1e6: 1.1e-16
  A: unique fixed point (Banach).  B: EVERY start in R^2 converges; no measure, no exclusions.
  C (exact rational arithmetic, n = 200): |x_a - x_b|_1 at n=200 = 4.56e-26 (initial 1e-6); exact inversion recovers x0_a: True
     float64 inversion after n =  20: recovery error 2.6e-14
     float64 inversion after n =  60: recovery error 7.2e-11
     float64 inversion after n = 120: recovery error 1.1e-04
     float64 inversion after n = 200: recovery error 1.1e+04
  => the affine contraction is a BIJECTION: fine-grained information is NOT erased; it is pushed below any
     finite resolution (A-priced forgetting).  Genuine erasure needs a non-injective law:
     F(x) = 0.8|x| + 0.5 (Lipschitz 0.8, non-injective): F(0.37) = 0.796, F(-0.37) = 0.796  -> merged EXACTLY in one step

